On Jul 11, 5:53 pm, eXcel <red.sec...@gmx.net> wrote:
> Hello there,
>
> I know that the domestic pets you start with grow larger as they level
> up, but I always thought this natural evolution stops when the pet has
> reached its "third form" (large cat, large dog or warhorse). Now
> something just happened that made me wonder:
>
> My current character, Gruumsh the dwarven caveman, was walking around
> the dungeon with his two large dogs Rex and Rix (both XL 9) in tow.
> He's fighting a horde of rothes in a corridor while the dogs are
> playing with the inhabitants of a large throne room on the other end
> of the level. Suddenly, a group of soldier ants shows up and Gruumsh
> decides to whistle in his dogs, just to be safe - and is quite
> surprised to see that Rix is now an XL 12 hell hound.
>
> Now I'm curious to know if that dog had simply killed enough things to
> level up some more and thus grow into a hell hound on its own, or if
> it's a weird coincidence that a polytrap/chameleon corpse turned my
> large dog into a hell hound (which just happens to fit nicely as the
> next step in a dog's evolution).
>
> Though I've been playing for years, I've never had any domestic pets
> last long enough (not in their original form, at least) to see if
> they'll eventually go past their large form, or XL9. Please enlighten
> me :)
>
> Greetings,
> C.   - eXcel

This change resulted from a polytrap. Large dogs/cats don't level past
XL9 on their own. If you wish to improve your pet without polymorphing
it, you can feed it wraith corpses, or give it potions of gain level
(if it's a humanoid). However, since large dogs and large cats can't
even kill a giant mimic reliably, I wouldn't recommend it. Tame a
purple worm and let it munch on wraiths in the valley or the priest
quest, and *then* you'll have a powerhouse. ;)



